    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n4767" root="ثنى" body-key="v">
    
      <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">ثَنَوِىٌّ</span></b>  rel. n. of <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">اِثْنَانِ</span>, and of <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">اِثْنَا عَشَرَ</span>, when
either or these is used as the proper name of a
man; as also ↓ <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">اِثْنِىٌّ</span></b>  [with <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">ٱ</span> when connected with
a preceding word]; like <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">بِنَوِىٌّ</span> and <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">اِبْنِىٌّ</span> as rel. ns.
of <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">اِبْنٌ</span>. (S.) ― 
       <p class="alpheios-lex-sense-separator"> </p><span class="alpheios-lex-sense-spacer2"> </span>-b2-  And <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">الثَّنَوِيَّةُ</span> [<i>The Dualists;</i>] <i>the
sect who assert the doctrine of Dualism</i> [<span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">الٱِثْنَيْنِيَّة</span>].
(TA.)

